from Datastore to Spanner.

00:07:00.409 --> 00:07:01.700
MINGLIANG WEI: Thanks, Niniane.

00:07:01.700 --> 00:07:04.650
So now, let's talk about
the database system

00:07:04.650 --> 00:07:08.600
that we use to
store player state.

00:07:08.600 --> 00:07:11.850
We started using Datastore
to store our player

00:07:11.850 --> 00:07:14.030
state since 2012
when we launched

00:07:14.030 --> 00:07:17.730
our first game, "Ingress."

00:07:17.730 --> 00:07:20.380
It has served us really well.

00:07:20.380 --> 00:07:24.630
In fact, we built our
second game, "Pokemon GO,"

00:07:24.630 --> 00:07:28.380
and launched it in 2016
on top of Datastore.

00:07:31.270 --> 00:07:36.010
Datastore is a
scalable NoSQL database

00:07:36.010 --> 00:07:39.400
that supports transactions.

00:07:39.400 --> 00:07:44.290
It is strongly consistent if
it's queried by primary key

00:07:44.290 --> 00:07:47.496
or by ancestors.

00:07:47.496 --> 00:07:51.710
However, the
secondary index, they

00:07:51.710 --> 00:07:53.170
are not strongly consistent.

00:07:53.170 --> 00:07:57.230
They are only
eventually consistent.

00:07:57.230 --> 00:08:00.920
By that, we mean
newly-created rows may not

00:08:00.920 --> 00:08:05.870
appear in the secondary
index immediately,

00:08:05.870 --> 00:08:09.410
and rows that are
modified or deleted

00:08:09.410 --> 00:08:12.260
may stay in the index for
a short period of time.

00:08:15.740 --> 00:08:20.570
As you can imagine, this could
cause a problem to our players.

00:08:20.570 --> 00:08:26.030
For example, in "Pokemon GO,"
we have this friendlist feature

00:08:26.030 --> 00:08:30.080
where you can add a
player as your friend.

00:08:30.080 --> 00:08:34.820
In order to do that, you need to
first create an InvitationCode,

00:08:34.820 --> 00:08:37.850
and the other player
needs to use this code

00:08:37.850 --> 00:08:41.309
to add you as a friend.

00:08:41.309 --> 00:08:44.730
And this InvitationCode actually
goes to the secondary index

00:08:44.730 --> 00:08:48.180
of the player table.

00:08:48.180 --> 00:08:51.810
If the secondary
index is behind,

00:08:51.810 --> 00:08:54.210
the other player wouldn't
be able to find you and use

00:08:54.210 --> 00:08:56.710
the same InvitationCode
you just provided to him.

00:08:59.380 --> 00:09:03.400
So the other player has to wait
until the secondary index is

00:09:03.400 --> 00:09:04.000
populated.

00:09:07.390 --> 00:09:12.100
So we have a lot of
experience on this.

00:09:12.100 --> 00:09:14.180
And we created a
workaround, which

00:09:14.180 --> 00:09:20.830
essentially is a new table that
functions as a secondary index.

00:09:20.830 --> 00:09:24.940
In this case, the table is
the InvitationCode table,

00:09:24.940 --> 00:09:27.460
and the primary key
is InvitationCode.

00:09:30.850 --> 00:09:33.040
For Datastore, if you
query by primary key,

00:09:33.040 --> 00:09:35.200
it's always strongly consistent.

00:09:35.200 --> 00:09:38.029
This actually solves the
problem of secondary index

00:09:38.029 --> 00:09:38.570
being behind.

00:09:42.000 --> 00:09:44.610
However, it also
brings some problems,

00:09:44.610 --> 00:09:48.180
because now we have the same
data in two separate tables.

00:09:51.830 --> 00:09:55.340
Whenever a game entity is
updated, a lot of the times,

00:09:55.340 --> 00:09:58.640
the secondary index table
has to be updated too.

00:10:01.440 --> 00:10:04.380
We build a lot of
code just to make

00:10:04.380 --> 00:10:06.930
sure the data in both
tables are consistent.

00:10:10.490 --> 00:10:12.660
And this is an
error-prone process.

00:10:12.660 --> 00:10:15.070
As you can imagine, as the
number of a secondary indexes

00:10:15.070 --> 00:10:19.280
increase, the amount of code
to deal with this problem

00:10:19.280 --> 00:10:21.420
is becoming unmanageable.

00:10:24.240 --> 00:10:27.510
So we are searching the
next generation data

00:10:27.510 --> 00:10:32.490
storage that can power our games
in the next five or 10 years.

00:10:32.490 --> 00:10:36.570
The following criteria
are important.

00:10:36.570 --> 00:10:38.290
Firstly, the cost.

00:10:38.290 --> 00:10:42.930
It has to be as
good as Datastore.

00:10:42.930 --> 00:10:52.070
In terms of latency, it must
be at least similar or better

00:10:52.070 --> 00:10:54.920
than Datastore.

00:10:54.920 --> 00:10:57.320
The ability to perform
relational queries

00:10:57.320 --> 00:11:00.500
is important too,
because with that,

00:11:00.500 --> 00:11:03.180
we can just use a single
query to do most things.

00:11:08.940 --> 00:11:10.940
Here is where Spanner
comes into the picture.

00:11:14.260 --> 00:11:19.040
Spanner is a new database
system that's offered by Google.

00:11:19.040 --> 00:11:23.320
It gets into GA
status back in 2017,

00:11:23.320 --> 00:11:26.785
and we keep a close eye on
this new database system.

00:11:29.970 --> 00:11:35.520
Compared with Datastore,
Spanner is also very scalable,

00:11:35.520 --> 00:11:39.930
and it's a SQL database,
which means it allows

00:11:39.930 --> 00:11:41.670
us to do aggregation joins.

00:11:45.190 --> 00:11:49.050
The secondary indexes in
Spanner are strongly consistent.

00:11:52.130 --> 00:11:54.380
Other things we really
liked about Spanner

00:11:54.380 --> 00:11:58.610
include a unique index and
the cascading deletion.

00:12:03.730 --> 00:12:05.980
Within the organization,
we had a lot

00:12:05.980 --> 00:12:09.580
of discussion about, what do
we do with this new storage

00:12:09.580 --> 00:12:10.870
system?

00:12:10.870 --> 00:12:13.330
Do we want to try
it and possibly

00:12:13.330 --> 00:12:16.540
use it to power all of our
products in the next five

00:12:16.540 --> 00:12:19.270
or 10 years?

00:12:19.270 --> 00:12:22.150
If yes, when do we start?

00:12:22.150 --> 00:12:23.190
How should we start it?

00:12:29.470 --> 00:12:33.970
The points from people
that are in doubt

00:12:33.970 --> 00:12:36.840
includes the following.

00:12:36.840 --> 00:12:40.180
Datastore works just fine
for the current games.

00:12:40.180 --> 00:12:45.540
Why are we spending so
scarce engineering resources

00:12:45.540 --> 00:12:47.700
to replace something
that is not broken?

00:12:52.060 --> 00:12:55.530
The organization has so
many tools and knowledge

00:12:55.530 --> 00:12:59.532
built around Datastore.

00:12:59.532 --> 00:13:02.990
We have zero on Spanner.

00:13:02.990 --> 00:13:06.100
Do we want to go through the
same process of learning again?

00:13:11.580 --> 00:13:14.460
Using a new database
system actually introduces

00:13:14.460 --> 00:13:20.850
a risk to new product launches,
because we have very little

00:13:20.850 --> 00:13:21.850
knowledge on the system.

00:13:25.630 --> 00:13:28.540
If we ever want to migrate
the data from the existing

00:13:28.540 --> 00:13:33.130
system to Spanner, it will be
a pretty complex engineering

00:13:33.130 --> 00:13:36.700
process, and there is a
risk associated with that.

00:13:40.390 --> 00:13:45.430
We also heard the following
from people who are supportive.

00:13:45.430 --> 00:13:49.580
Working around the secondary
index being stale problem

00:13:49.580 --> 00:13:54.970
is just becoming so difficult.
The number of secondary indexes

00:13:54.970 --> 00:13:58.640
are becoming more and more.

00:13:58.640 --> 00:14:01.210
And the amount of code that
just to deal with this problem

00:14:01.210 --> 00:14:06.640
becoming unmanageable.

00:14:06.640 --> 00:14:10.300
This is the one opportunity
we can advance our database

00:14:10.300 --> 00:14:13.270
system, our database
technology, to make

00:14:13.270 --> 00:14:14.680
our system a lot simpler.

00:14:20.050 --> 00:14:22.800
We heard Spanner
has a great latency.

00:14:22.800 --> 00:14:24.422
I think we should just try it.

00:14:29.600 --> 00:14:31.850
As a data scientist,
I am so excited to be

00:14:31.850 --> 00:14:37.910
able to use SQL query to get
player intels from Spanner,

00:14:37.910 --> 00:14:41.030
which otherwise, I
have to ask engineers

00:14:41.030 --> 00:14:44.060
to write dataflow to get those
information from Datastore.

00:14:47.460 --> 00:14:49.320
This is a new Google product.

00:14:49.320 --> 00:14:53.250
We are going to get a lot
of help from Google side.

00:14:58.140 --> 00:15:00.590
A note on the data migration--

00:15:00.590 --> 00:15:04.950
so if we ever want to
decide to migrate existing

00:15:04.950 --> 00:15:12.000
data to Spanner, we are talking
about moving terabytes of data

00:15:12.000 --> 00:15:14.650
while they are being
changed by active players

00:15:14.650 --> 00:15:16.630
at the same time.

00:15:16.630 --> 00:15:20.590
This is, again, a very complex
engineering process, because we

00:15:20.590 --> 00:15:23.650
need to make sure all of
the data between Datastore

00:15:23.650 --> 00:15:25.870
and Spanner are in sync.

00:15:25.870 --> 00:15:27.715
And we can't afford
any downtime.

00:15:35.430 --> 00:15:40.410
After a lot of discussion
about tradeoffs and risks,

00:15:40.410 --> 00:15:45.060
we have decided to start with
a low-QPS system to experiment

00:15:45.060 --> 00:15:45.780
with Spanner.

00:15:48.740 --> 00:15:51.020
It is a lot easier
to get agreement

00:15:51.020 --> 00:15:53.660
for one experiment
with rollback options

00:15:53.660 --> 00:15:56.495
than committing to a
long-term migration strategy.

00:16:01.840 --> 00:16:07.460
The system that we chose
was the account system.

00:16:07.460 --> 00:16:10.260
The account system
is relatively simple.

00:16:13.140 --> 00:16:16.310
It has a lower
QPS than the game.

00:16:16.310 --> 00:16:18.370
And most of the
queries are read-only.

00:16:21.470 --> 00:16:25.490
We have this upcoming feature
called account linking, which

00:16:25.490 --> 00:16:29.390
allows the player to log in
with both Google and Facebook

00:16:29.390 --> 00:16:31.600
credentials.

00:16:31.600 --> 00:16:33.950
If we could build this
on top of Spanner,

00:16:33.950 --> 00:16:37.250
we can do it quickly,
because we no longer

00:16:37.250 --> 00:16:39.440
need to write code to deal
with the secondary index

00:16:39.440 --> 00:16:40.415
being stale problem.

00:16:44.740 --> 00:16:47.550
There's also risks.

00:16:47.550 --> 00:16:50.870
The account system is one
of the most critical systems

00:16:50.870 --> 00:16:53.390
that we have.

00:16:53.390 --> 00:16:55.760
If the account system
is not working,

00:16:55.760 --> 00:16:57.245
nobody can play our games.

00:17:02.770 --> 00:17:06.760
Relying on data
migration for Spanner

00:17:06.760 --> 00:17:09.130
also may delay the launch
of account linking.

00:17:13.890 --> 00:17:18.150
The account system manages all
of the codenames and logins

00:17:18.150 --> 00:17:20.650
for all of our games.

00:17:20.650 --> 00:17:22.990
It also helps to locate
players for admin tools.

00:17:26.250 --> 00:17:30.020
It is constantly
running on App Engine.

00:17:30.020 --> 00:17:33.702
And before the migration,
the storage is Datastore.

00:17:33.702 --> 00:17:35.160
We are going to
move it to Spanner.

00:17:39.940 --> 00:17:43.870
Now is the data
migration process.

00:17:43.870 --> 00:17:46.215
It is roughly divided
into three steps.

00:17:50.170 --> 00:17:53.610
The first step is
copying the data,

00:17:53.610 --> 00:17:55.320
which means copying
all of the accounts

00:17:55.320 --> 00:17:57.220
from Datastore to Spanner.

00:18:00.630 --> 00:18:04.230
The second step is validation.

00:18:04.230 --> 00:18:09.840
In this step, we validate data
between the two repositories

00:18:09.840 --> 00:18:11.660
and make sure they
are actually in sync.

00:18:15.220 --> 00:18:18.720
The third step is switching
Spanner and making

00:18:18.720 --> 00:18:21.560
it the source of truth.

00:18:21.560 --> 00:18:26.065
All of the data we return to
the player will be from Spanner.

00:18:32.940 --> 00:18:35.460
Step one, copying the data.

00:18:39.620 --> 00:18:45.660
During this step, we are
copying terabytes of data

00:18:45.660 --> 00:18:50.250
at the same time they are being
modified by active players.

00:18:50.250 --> 00:18:52.470
So the first thing
we need to do is

00:18:52.470 --> 00:18:55.640
whatever change is made
by the active players,

00:18:55.640 --> 00:18:58.035
we want the change to
be populated to Spanner.

00:19:01.140 --> 00:19:05.160
To achieve this, we
used dual writes.

00:19:05.160 --> 00:19:07.980
We created a
transaction listener

00:19:07.980 --> 00:19:11.370
in the data access
layer of Datastore

00:19:11.370 --> 00:19:15.630
so that whenever account
changes by an active player,

00:19:15.630 --> 00:19:17.320
we copy the whole
account to Spanner.

00:19:19.920 --> 00:19:23.440
So this takes care of the
changes made by active players.

00:19:27.100 --> 00:19:28.530
What about the inactive players?

00:19:31.610 --> 00:19:35.750
We run a dataflow to copy
all accounts from Dataflow

00:19:35.750 --> 00:19:36.400
to Spanner.

00:19:39.050 --> 00:19:43.160
Wait, are there any
race conditions?

00:19:43.160 --> 00:19:47.210
Yes, because the
same data in Spanner

00:19:47.210 --> 00:19:50.920
could be modified
by both transaction

00:19:50.920 --> 00:19:54.540
listener and the dataflow
at the same time.

00:19:54.540 --> 00:19:56.840
So there could be
race conditions.

00:19:56.840 --> 00:20:02.480
To resolve this, we
carefully wrote the dataflow

00:20:02.480 --> 00:20:05.620
to use an insert mutation.

00:20:05.620 --> 00:20:09.650
Insert mutation fails
if the account already

00:20:09.650 --> 00:20:12.680
exists on Spanner.

00:20:12.680 --> 00:20:16.790
This situation happens when
the active player has already

00:20:16.790 --> 00:20:20.180
made changes to the account,
and therefore, the account

00:20:20.180 --> 00:20:22.640
is copied by the transaction
listener to Spanner.

00:20:26.040 --> 00:20:29.940
So during this process, we
continue monitor Spanner

00:20:29.940 --> 00:20:33.170
latency and throughput
to make sure Spanner

00:20:33.170 --> 00:20:37.110
is working fine with the
traffic from the dataflow.

00:20:39.990 --> 00:20:43.530
By the way, the dual writes
can be turned off if needed.

00:20:48.020 --> 00:20:51.980
Step two is data validation.

00:20:51.980 --> 00:20:55.100
This step lasted for two weeks.

00:20:55.100 --> 00:20:59.530
During this two weeks, we ran
validation multiple times.

00:21:03.110 --> 00:21:07.280
The validation is
done by Dataflow.

00:21:07.280 --> 00:21:10.730
The Dataflow reads
accounts, all accounts

00:21:10.730 --> 00:21:13.970
from Datastore and Spanner
and then compares them.

00:21:16.590 --> 00:21:22.130
We do expect some
inconsistent results.

00:21:22.130 --> 00:21:26.900
The reason for that is just
because the accounts reading

00:21:26.900 --> 00:21:33.180
from Datastore and Spanner may
come from different timestamps.

00:21:33.180 --> 00:21:37.090
For these inconsistent
results, we

00:21:37.090 --> 00:21:40.400
will sample them and
manually check them to make

00:21:40.400 --> 00:21:41.720
sure they are actually in sync.

00:21:45.910 --> 00:21:47.900
At the end of the
second week, we

00:21:47.900 --> 00:21:51.620
are very confident that the
data between two repositories

00:21:51.620 --> 00:21:52.480
are in sync.

00:21:56.580 --> 00:22:01.220
Now, we are ready to switch
Spanner as the source of truth.

00:22:01.220 --> 00:22:03.860
We still keep
writing to Datastore,

00:22:03.860 --> 00:22:07.640
because we want the option
to roll back to Datastore

00:22:07.640 --> 00:22:09.900
if anything goes wrong.

00:22:09.900 --> 00:22:13.100
We keep writing to Datastore
for another two weeks.

00:22:13.100 --> 00:22:16.100
During this two weeks,
we keep monitoring

00:22:16.100 --> 00:22:20.430
Spanner bug reports,
stackdriver counters,

00:22:20.430 --> 00:22:23.690
[INAUDIBLE],, whatever
source we can

00:22:23.690 --> 00:22:26.690
find if there's any issue
with the new system running

00:22:26.690 --> 00:22:29.180
on Spanner.

00:22:29.180 --> 00:22:32.900
And the whole migration
process from the beginning

00:22:32.900 --> 00:22:37.050
to the end of step three
lasted for four weeks.

00:22:41.600 --> 00:22:44.110
At the end of the
fourth week, we

00:22:44.110 --> 00:22:46.540
are very confident that the
new system works properly

00:22:46.540 --> 00:22:47.470
on Spanner.

00:22:47.470 --> 00:22:50.641
Now we can turn off the
writes to Datastore.

00:22:53.470 --> 00:22:55.390
With that, we can
no longer go back

00:22:55.390 --> 00:22:57.730
to Datastore easily,
because the changes are not

00:22:57.730 --> 00:22:59.451
populated there anymore.

00:23:03.140 --> 00:23:05.870
Thanks to Google,
we got a lot of help

00:23:05.870 --> 00:23:10.790
from Google Cloud support
team during the migration.

00:23:10.790 --> 00:23:12.980
Particularly, we
received guidance

00:23:12.980 --> 00:23:20.360
on performance regarding schema
design, indexing, and query.

00:23:20.360 --> 00:23:24.860
We got early access to the
open source Spanner backup

00:23:24.860 --> 00:23:28.310
tool, which allows us to
have peace of mind knowing

00:23:28.310 --> 00:23:32.150
that if anything goes wrong, we
can still go back to an older

00:23:32.150 --> 00:23:34.240
version of the data.

00:23:34.240 --> 00:23:38.430
Google Cloud support team also
monitored our Spanner instance

00:23:38.430 --> 00:23:41.960
during the migration to
make sure the instance is

00:23:41.960 --> 00:23:44.150
performant, is working properly.

00:23:48.960 --> 00:23:53.030
So this is four weeks of
hard work to copy the data

00:23:53.030 --> 00:23:56.060
and validate it.

00:23:56.060 --> 00:24:02.860
This is the result. We
are very happy to see we

00:24:02.860 --> 00:24:04.930
have a lot better performance.

00:24:04.930 --> 00:24:11.300
In terms of latency, it's
25% better at 50 percentile.

00:24:11.300 --> 00:24:15.880
And we are very excited to see
there's a cost savings as well.

00:24:18.590 --> 00:24:21.670
The reason behind
that is for Datastore,

00:24:21.670 --> 00:24:24.890
it is charged by
number of accesses.

00:24:24.890 --> 00:24:28.690
However, for Spanner, it's
charged by number of nodes.

00:24:32.590 --> 00:24:35.170
After this data migration,
the whole company just

00:24:35.170 --> 00:24:38.230
has a lot more confidence
on the new database system,

00:24:38.230 --> 00:24:40.960
and we are ready to move
more systems to Spanner.

00:24:45.160 --> 00:24:49.300
On top of Spanner, we launched
account linking in March

00:24:49.300 --> 00:24:52.210
and friendlist in June.

00:24:52.210 --> 00:24:54.500
In particular, for
the friendlist launch,

00:24:54.500 --> 00:24:57.010
we modified our data access
layer to work with Spanner.

00:24:59.740 --> 00:25:03.550
This allows us to move
existing systems to Spanner,

00:25:03.550 --> 00:25:06.760
switching the data storage
layer from Datastore

00:25:06.760 --> 00:25:09.430
to Spanner with just
changing a few lines of code.

00:25:16.600 --> 00:25:18.510
Here's the QPS to Spanner.

00:25:18.510 --> 00:25:21.750
The red line is
from account system,

00:25:21.750 --> 00:25:23.835
and the blue line is from
the friendlist system.

00:25:27.080 --> 00:25:32.670
The social platform team
launched account system

00:25:32.670 --> 00:25:36.330
on top of Spanner to show how
to migrate data from existing

00:25:36.330 --> 00:25:37.350
system to Spanner.

00:25:40.020 --> 00:25:42.750
And we launched the friendlist
system on top of Spanner

00:25:42.750 --> 00:25:47.340
to show that Spanner is
performant and scalable

00:25:47.340 --> 00:25:50.395
to support live game
traffic from "Pokemon GO."

00:25:56.080 --> 00:25:59.260
After the two
experiments on Spanner,

00:25:59.260 --> 00:26:01.930
the whole company
is very excited.

00:26:01.930 --> 00:26:06.220
A lot of teams decided to
move their product to Spanner

00:26:06.220 --> 00:26:07.840
on their own.

00:26:07.840 --> 00:26:10.670
We now have one
team in the process

00:26:10.670 --> 00:26:13.090
of moving their product,
and another team

00:26:13.090 --> 00:26:14.470
is in the planning stage.

00:26:17.570 --> 00:26:22.370
So we found that instead
of continued debate,

00:26:22.370 --> 00:26:26.090
the best way to convince is
start with a small experiment

00:26:26.090 --> 00:26:27.350
and expand from there.

00:26:34.100 --> 00:26:38.360
Here are the takeaways
from this talk.

00:26:38.360 --> 00:26:43.050
For data migration, we
recommend the following steps.

00:26:43.050 --> 00:26:44.860
Start with copying
and dual writes.

00:26:47.660 --> 00:26:50.840
Do the write validation.

00:26:50.840 --> 00:26:54.950
Switch the application
to the new storage.

00:26:54.950 --> 00:26:57.020
And finally, turn
off original writes.

00:27:01.130 --> 00:27:03.920
Always have a backup plan--

00:27:03.920 --> 00:27:06.040
a rollback plan.

00:27:06.040 --> 00:27:09.680
With that, we can always
go back to the state

00:27:09.680 --> 00:27:14.250
before the migration
if anything goes wrong.

00:27:14.250 --> 00:27:17.270
The one way to convince
the organization

00:27:17.270 --> 00:27:25.220
to do an experiment is we
start from a low-traffic system

00:27:25.220 --> 00:27:26.300
and expand from there.

00:27:26.300 --> 00:27:30.550
[MUSIC PLAYING]

